          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The consensus recognizes two major fish-symbolism strands: biblically, fish represent people gathered through mission and ultimately separated in judgment; historically and iconographically, the fish also represents Christ and Christian identity through the Ichthys emblem.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book identifies a fish as a person among the nations who is caught through gospel mission and sorted as clean or unclean at the final harvest.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Fish represent people caught or gathered through mission and subject to final separation as righteous or wicked.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"A fish is a person in the sea of nations, gathered by fishers and finally sorted as clean or unclean.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement appears within the people-as-fish sense. The book combines the evangelistic catch with the dragnet's inclusion and sorting of good and bad fish. It does not discuss the separate Christ/Ichthys, provision, or Jonah senses recognized by consensus, but omission of those contextual alternatives does not alter this entry's core referent.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book's identification directly corresponds to the consensus's biblical-exegetical strand concerning people gathered through mission and judgment.\",\"rationale\":\"Matthew 4:19 explicitly equates the objects of fishing with people, while Matthew 13:47-48 depicts fish of every kind being gathered and divided into good and bad. Habakkuk 1:14 also directly compares human beings to fish of the sea. The book's language of persons, gathering, and sorting therefore restates a well-recognized consensus identification rather than replacing or materially refining it.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Several consensus responses treat Christ via the post-biblical Ichthys acrostic as the dominant general Christian meaning, whereas the book presents only the people-as-fish sense. If the entry were intended to exclude all other contextual meanings universally, it could be viewed as narrower; however, its cited biblical context squarely matches the established people-and-judgment reading.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
